WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.480
[Dr. John Pool:] Cancer is the most life-threatening situation that children face 

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.840
after neonatal period, except for accidents. 

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.910
At the 1968 meeting of the American Radium Society, 

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.560
one aspect of pediatric cancer is on the program for this reason. 

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:35.470
And also, because the treatment of solid tumors in children 

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:43.540
requires the joint services of surgeon, radiotherapist, and chemotherapist. 

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.260
And all three of these disciplines are represented in our membership.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.770
[Music]

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:57.230
[American Radium Society]

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:04.010
[Narrator:] As a professional education extension of the 50th annual meeting, the American Radium Society presents Childhood Cancer: Current Outlook. 

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:12.390
Participants are Dr. Lois Murphy, Chairman of the Department of Pediatrics at Memorial Hospital in New York; 

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:17.860
[Lois Murphy, M.D., Chairman, Dept. of Pediatrics, Memorial Hospital, New York]

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.930
Dr. Lester W. Martin, Director of Pediatric Surgery at the Children's Hospital in Cincinnati, Ohio; 

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:27.020
[Lester W. Martin, M.D., Director of Pediatric Surgery, Children's Hospital, Cincinnati] 

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.770
Dr. Melvin Tefft, Radiotherapist and Chief at the Children's Hospital in Boston; 

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.610
[Melvin Tefft, M.D., Radiotherapist-in-Chief, Children's Hospital, Boston]

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:41.450
and as moderator, Dr. Lemuel Bowden, Associate Attending Surgeon at Memorial Hospital in New York City.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:46.580
[Lemuel Bowden, M.D., Associate Attending Surgeon, Memorial Hospital, New York]

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:55.870
[Dr. Lemuel Bowden:] Obviously in the time allotted, it would be impossible to comment even briefly on every type of solid malignant tumor of childhood. 

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:06.220
And we have agreed to confine our remarks to three of the most common ones, namely Wilms tumor, neuroblastoma, and embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma. 

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:16.230
The first case that I'd like to present is that of a nine-month-old baby boy who was noted approximately one month before hospital admission

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:23.370
to have a slowly increasing abdominal girth with some distention of the superficial veins of the abdomen. 

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:28.320
But was not noted to have an abdominal mass until the day prior to admission. 

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:35.360
An immediate IVP was secured and this revealed the presence of a lesion of the right kidney. 

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:42.640
On the basis of this brief history, Dr. Martin, how would you go about managing this case?

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:48.240
[Dr. Lester Martin:] This particular Wilms tumor is an ideal candidate for immediate object removal. 

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:54.620
The origin of the tumor from the lower part of the kidney tends to displace the renal pedicle upward somewhat

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:59.960
and creates a bit more of a difficult operative approach for the surgeon.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:05.290
A point could be made for a thoracoabdominal approach in this particular child. 

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:13.300
Ordinarily as seen in the photograph, we prefer a transverse abdominal incision extending from one flank to the other,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:21.650
providing generous exposure and permitting early operation of the renal pedicle in the course of the operation.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:29.780
The location of the tumor within the kidney is of more importance to the surgeon than is the size.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:36.070
As seen in the next photograph, a particularly large tumor may be removed with relative ease. 

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:42.200
We feel that during the course of the operation the opposite kidney should be examined carefully. 

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:49.840
And as shown in the next photograph, the tumor on the opposite side may be particularly small

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.870
and can be detected only by careful examination of all aspects of the opposite kidney.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:01.830
Bilateral involvement can be expected in approximately ten percent of the cases with Wilms tumor. 

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:10.100
We see that the operation is not complete until a thorough retroperitoneal [inaudible] assessment has been carried out. 

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:18.080
Occasionally the tumors spread to the retroperitoneal nodes prior to its spread to the lungs or elsewhere.

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.790
[Dr. Bowden:] In an early case such as this one we have just detailed, 

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:27.530
do you feel, Dr. Tefft, that there is any case for radiation therapy, 

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:35.880
and possibly you'd also comment on the possibility of finding positive nodes or extrarenal extension

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:43.040
at the time of surgical exploration and thereby, or in such an instance, a need for radiation therapy?

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:51.820
[Dr. Tefft:] Well, in the younger child, especially a child under 13 months of age with a good overall prognosis, 

00:04:51.820 --> 00:05:00.740
when the tumor is well-encapsulated and with no evidence of breaking the capsule, when there is no lymph node involvement and when there is no local vascular invasion,

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:05.150
it is not our policy to use routine postoperative irradiation. 

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:13.420
However, when any of these other things are true; when these things are positive at the time of initial surgery or in the older child, 

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:17.440
when radiotherapy is used, the volume is carefully tailored to encompass

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.080
the tumor-bearing tissue and to minimize the radiation to normal growth structures. 

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:30.040
The dose is consistent with both control of the tumor, and adjusted to the tolerance of normal tissues in the growing child.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:36.740
[Dr. Bowden:] Dr. Murphy, in an early Wilms tumor, what place is there for chemotherapy?

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:43.080
[Dr. Lois Murphy:] A commonly accepted practice is to start a course of Actinomycin D on the day of operation. 

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.700
[Actinomycin D. day of surgery, invervals thereafter, concurrent irratiation +-]

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:52.060
Many centers then give repeated courses of Actinomycin D at intervals of six weeks to two or three months, for one year. 

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:56.300
In our institution, the first two doses of Actinomycin D

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:02.180
are given before operation together with two doses of radiotherapy over the tumor. 

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:09.930
Following operation, the remaining three doses of Actinomycin D is given and the radiotherapy of course continued. 

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:16.760
Repeated courses of Actinomycin D are given at intervals of two to three months for one to two years.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:25.160
[Dr. Bowden:] Of course, the idea behind such therapy is to prevent, we hope, the possibility of metastases. 

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:37.310
If these metastases, however, do occur in a Wilms Tumor, does this mean invariably a completely hopeless prognosis, Dr. Murphy?

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:44.080
[Dr. Murphy:] No, there are many children who have received chemotherapy and radiotherapy

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:51.050
for metastatic Wilms Tumor who had disappearance of these conditions and are well at the present time.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.730
[Dr. Bowden:] And is there any choice, do you think,

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:58.740
between the chemotherapy and the radiation therapy in the management of such metastatic lesions? 

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.270
Or do you think it's an entirely a combined, a combined endeavor?

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:12.110
[Dr. Murphy:] I think that it should be a combined endeavor and of course the lesions may be excised surgically if they are discrete.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:16.760
[Dr. Bowden:] I was going to ask that of Dr. Martin. 

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:25.050
In the case of a solitary pulmonary metastasis from Wilms Tumor, do you think it's very often that it is in fact solitary

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:35.400
and that there is a place then for a surgical approach and segmental excision or lobectomy such as a solitary metastasis?

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.900
[Dr. Martin:] In answer to your first question, I doubt that any of them are solitary.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.760
I suspect that most of them are multiple. 

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:50.910
We feel that the initial metastatic lesions can be treated by a combination of radiation plus chemotherapy. 

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:55.630
In occasional instance, one single lesion will recur following this course of treatment

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:59.050
or will persist in spite of combined therapy. 

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:02.240
In that instance we do it by surgical removal.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.540
And in case another one should recur later on, we would remove that. 

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:08.860
We've had one child that has had five separate resections,

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:13.430
removing only a small amount of lung tissue with each particular tumor,

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:18.170
and the child has now had five, over five years since the last resection.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:23.410
[Dr. Bowden:] Dr. Tefft, would you care to comment on the management of metastatic Wilms tumor?

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:26.410
[Dr. Tefft:] I would agree completely with Dr. Murphy that the first attack, 

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:33.950
and Dr. Martin, that the first attack would be a combination of radiotherapy and chemotherapy for the first evidence of metastasis. 

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:39.400
And certainly there are some children who do have solitary lesions that have survived after excision. 

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:48.440
I think the only point to make is when one does irradiate the lung for metastasis, one includes the total volume of both lungs, both apices and bases.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:55.950
[Dr. Bowden:] First case, being a relatively early example of Wilms Tumor, probably carries a very good prognosis with it. 

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:59.910
Unfortunately, this is not the case with all childhood tumors. 

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:06.880
And for the next case I'd like to present that of a seven-month-old baby girl

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:14.700
who was found some two months before admission to the hospital to have likewise an enlarging abdomen

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:20.720
and was noted to have an abdominal mass in the left upper quadrant shortly before admission,

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:25.940
at the same time as a mass in the left groin was identified. 

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:34.320
The small mass in the left groin was removed for histologic study and proved to be metastatic neuroblastoma. 

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:40.350
An IVP [inaudible] revealed marked distortion of the left kidney position

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:47.030
because of the presence of a large suprarenal mass with flaky calcification contained therein.

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:52.260
Furthermore, a marrow aspiration in this child revealed the presence of extrinsic cells

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:55.770
consistent with metastatic neuroblastoma in the marrow. 

00:09:55.770 --> 00:10:02.290
Although the skeletal survey showed no definite evidence of bone destruction.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:09.850
In a situation of this sort, Dr. Tefft, would you comment as to how you would approach the problem therapeutically?

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:12.180
[Dr. Tefft:] Well, in a child under one year of age,

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:17.170
it is a distinctly better prognosis as compared to the older child with neuroblastoma. 

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:26.200
Given such evidence of disseminary disease, every attempt is made to control a disease that is encompassable by radiation

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:29.070
and at doses tolerable to normal growing tissue. 

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:35.230
Therefore, after biopsy and possibly after surgical removal of the suprarenal primary,

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:43.090
we would advocate irradiation of the suprarenal fossa and the node-bearing areas in continuity with this involved groin node. 

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:47.950
In advanced cases, in older children, when there is [inaudible] bone destruction by x-ray,

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:52.960
we would deliver more modest curative doses of irradiation to the local site.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:11:05.280
[Dr. Bowden:] May I ask Dr. Martin, would you consider immediate surgery on the primary tumor above the left kidney, as a surgeon? 

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:11.680
Or would you prefer possibly to ask your radiation therapist colleague to give preliminary radiation therapy

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:17.310
before attacking this surgically, or would you attack the primary tumor surgically at all?

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:28.440
[Dr. Lester Martin:] In this particular child with evidence of metastatic disease in the bone marrow, we would be less, feel less urgent about object removal. 

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:34.330
If we had a negative bone marrow we would approach this aggressively and go immediately and remove the tumor. 

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:39.590
With a positive bone marrow, we would discuss it, the members of the team...

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:48.450
and come to a decision and I suspect that we would probably remove the primary and then use combined radiation and chemotherapy.

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:52.930
[Dr. Bowden:] Chemotherapy. And Dr. Murphy now. 

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:58.970
The question of the involvement of bone marrow has been utmost in the minds of all us in this particular patient. 

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:07.120
Would you comment on the significance of this and the treatment of this chemotherapeutically if you feel this is what should be now done.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:11.090
[Dr. Murphy:] I'd like to re-emphasize what Dr. Tefft said, 

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:15.240
that the prognosis in such a patient is grave but not hopeless. 

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:19.810
Neuroblastoma invading bone marrow has similarities to acute leukemia. 

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:26.690
Treatment should be selected to eradicate the extrinsic cell, thereafter normal bone marrow cells return.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:32.250
Neuroblastoma cells can be suppressed within a few days by the more vigorous chemotherapeutic agents. 

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:36.790
For example, a full dose of nitrogen mustard, a course of Actinomycin D., or high doses of cyclophosphamide. 

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:40.000
[Treatment: Nitrogen Mustard, Actinomycin D., Cyclophosphamide]

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:44.220
There may result hyperuricemia; temporary, severe thrombocytopenia; and leukopenia. 

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.510
[Complications: Hyperuricemia, Thrombocytopenia, Leukopenia]

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:54.310
To treat with these agents, the child needs to be prepared with good hydration and Allopurinol to prevent hyperuricemia. 

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:57.820
[Prepare with: 1) good hydration, 2) Allopurinal, Treat with: 1) Platelet transfusion, 2) (Isoluation)]

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:02.980
In addition, if bleeding develops, platelet concentrates for supportive management may be required. 

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:08.570
If leukopenia is severe enough to lower the circulating granulocyte count to 1,500,

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:13.890
a strict isolation to guard against infection may be necessary. 

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:17.510
And a normal bone marrow should regenerate within two or three weeks,

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:21.870
following which a maintenance schedule of weekly, alternating doses of Cyclophosphamide and Vincristine can be used. 

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:25.320
[Alternating weekly doses: Cyclophosphamide, Vincristine]

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:29.680
If one is not able to give heroic supportive therapy to such a patient,

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:36.060
the latter two drugs can be used as the initial treatment and some good results have been observed with them. 

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:41.750
Some kind of chemotherapy certainly should be given for probably two years. 

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:47.460
The catecholamine excretion in the urine, as well as careful physical examination

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:56.590
and serial bone marrow examinations, are helpful in determining activity of the neuroblastoma during the course of treatment.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:14:02.670
[Dr. Bowden:] Knowing of course, the hematopoietic depression effect in most chemotherapeutic agents,

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:10.840
I think some practitioners might rightly fear the use of massive doses of any of the chemotherapeutic drugs

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:16.500
if it appeared that the marrow had been largely replaced by extrinsic tumor cells. 

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:23.470
Would you comment on that? Do you think that is a contraindicating factor? The degree of marrow replacement in other words?

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:28.700
[Dr. Murphy:] Well, this fear is certainly justified and we all share it. 

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:40.320
However, these neuroblastoma cells must be removed from the bone marrow, before there can be a remission and return of normal elements. 

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:44.770
So it takes a good deal of clinical judgment in determining how much of an agent

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:49.950
a young patient can tolerate. It has something to do with the marrow reserve in a given child.

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:54.550
[Dr. Bowden:] One comment I think certainly should be made with regard to neuroblastoma

00:14:54.550 --> 00:15:02.250
is the fact that in occasional instances there will be of course spontaneous regression of the tumor that is currently not completely understood. 

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:11.190
And for that reason too, I think that a totally hopeless prognosis should not be given, even in the advanced case such as we've just seen. 

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:14.950
So I'd like to go on to our third case if I may. 

00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:21.250
This is that of a ten-year-old boy who three weeks prior to admission to the hospital

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:28.270
was noted by his mother to have a painless mass in the right arm, affixed to the bone,

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:32.570
or seemingly affixed to the bone and just distal to the elbow. 

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:40.140
He was seen by his local physician and promptly referred to the hospital on the suspicion that this might be a malignant tumor. 

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:48.540
At the time of admission, this mass was noted to be a fairly good size; five by three [inaudible] infiltrating mass. 

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:58.970
And at the same time, two or three definitely enlarged lymph nodes were identified in the axilla of that side. 

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:05.990
Assuming, as was the case in this patient, that the lesion proved to be an embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma,

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:11.440
would you comment Dr. Martin, on your approach to this problem?

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:14.040
[Dr. Martin:] This is a particularly difficult problem. 

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:21.820
The fixation of the mass to the bony structure of the forearm precludes local excision

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:30.330
as a means of treating this as a [inaudible] surgical procedure unless we are quite radical in our approach. 

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:34.720
The spread to the axilla again means we must be more radical

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:40.300
I think the only way we can approach this surgically other than to do the biopsy would be to do a forequarter amputation,

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:51.130
which is certainly a rather mutilating operation for a small child faced with 65 more years of going around with the forequarter amputated. 

00:16:51.130 --> 00:17:02.230
Now I would choose between that, or biopsy only and use the other modalities such as either radiation or chemotherapy as far as the definitive management. 

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:08.530
I believe the only operation we could do would be a forequarter amputation as a curative type of procedure.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:16.830
[Dr. Bowden:] I'm glad to hear you say that because I think cure is possible, even in this particular presentation by such a radical surgery. 

00:17:16.830 --> 00:17:23.440
But I'd like to ask Dr. Tefft if he feels there's any place for radiation therapy in the management of this particular patient.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:28.030
[Dr. Tefft:] Well, as Dr. Martin has indicated this is indeed a very difficult problem.

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:37.990
Irradiation at high dose, especially when combined with chemotherapy, has resulted in marked regression of some of these lesions. 

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:46.770
But when the disease can be removed surgically, lest there be local recurrence and one cannot be sure of total eradication of all cells

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:52.910
in this particular region, it might be as well to do such a radical procedure as Dr. Martin has indicated. 

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:58.870
In other areas such as the nasopharynx when total surgical extirpation is usually not possible,

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:05.340
then radical high dose irradiation when combined with chemotherapy has indeed resulted in the survival in some patients.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:20.080
[Dr. Bowden:] Dr. Murphy, would you comment on the seeming influence of primary location of the embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma and prognosis or possibly in the treatment?

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:25.180
[Dr. Murphy:] Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma may arise in soft parts of the head and neck, the genitourinary tract, the extremities or the retroperitoneal areas. 

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:29.960
[Head and neck, Peripheral soft parts, Bladder-Prostate-Vagina, Abdomen]

00:18:29.960 --> 00:18:35.700
The prognosis is somewhat better when the origin of the tumor is in the head and neck area. 

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:43.110
Chemotherapy of choice for embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is Actinomycin D, Cyclophosphamide and Vincristine also has some beneficial effect. 

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:47.000
[Actinomycin D., Cyclophosphamide, Vincristine]

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:56.450
This tumor invades the bone marrow and therefore marrow examination needs to be carried out before the chemotherapy is selected.

00:18:56.450 --> 00:19:02.470
[Dr. Bowden:] And in this particular instance may I ask, assuming that a forequarter amputation were done,

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:10.720
would you recommend in the post-operative period that any you know, so-called suppressive chemotherapy be administered? 

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:16.380
This is a leading question but I'd be interested in your thoughts on that.

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:24.600
[Dr. Murphy:] Well, the frequency with which this tumor metastasizes widely, including replacing the bone marrow, 

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:30.630
I think it's worthwhile to consider so-called prophylactic chemotherapy in this condition,

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:38.500
give courses of Actinomycin D or perhaps I think this is a good situation to use combined chemotherapy.

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:47.860
There's been some interest in Actinomycin D together with Vincristine and we've given Actinomycin D together with Cyclophosphamide in repeated courses in an effort [inaudible];

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:49.780
[Dr. Bowden:] [Inaudible]

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:53.000
[Dr. Murphy:] in order to continuously suppress the tumor.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:54.030
[Dr. Bowden:] I see.

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.860
[Dr. Murphy:] Certainly along with the x-ray therapy and surgery.

00:19:56.860 --> 00:20:00.230
[Dr. Bowden:] Dr. Martin, would you agree with that? Or did you have any other comments?

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:02.100
[Dr. Martin:] No, I think I'd agree with that. 

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:05.310
In one particular child with rhabdomyosarcoma of the orbit

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:13.950
with metastasis to the local area was treated with a radical operation excising the orbit, primary occlusion of the tissues. 

00:20:13.950 --> 00:20:24.560
The metastatic lesions were removed by only local removal and the child received [inaudible] radiation to the neck and the nodes disappeared completely. 

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:27.500
It's only been 19 months since the procedure.

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:37.820
We still feel that that the prognosis is poor, but such a hopeful occurrence makes us feel as though it's not an entirely hopeless disease.

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:45.530
[Dr. Bowden:] I think many of us have the impression that possibly, embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma arising in the head and neck area

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:51.810
may be a little more responsive to radiation therapy than such tumors arising elsewhere,

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:57.780
and may have a little better prognosis, although this is very difficult to prove statistically. 

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:02.610
Would you care to comment on that, Dr. Tefft? Do you think they have a somewhat better prognosis?

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:08.570
[Dr. Tefft:] I think that I agree that it is difficult to analyze it, particularly as the patient numbers are so small.

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:11.840
But my impression is that this is indeed true.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:18.510
[Dr. Bowden:] Are there any other comments any of you would care to make about this case? 

00:21:18.510 --> 00:21:28.000
If not, in summary then, I think we have presented three fairly typical cases of solid malignant tumors in childhood

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:32.640
and their treatment has been most ably discussed by my panel members here.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:43.860
I think it is worthwhile to note that current practice favors the combined therapeutic approach for which the capabilities of several medical disciplines,

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:53.880
surgery, radiation therapy, and chemotherapy, may be and frequently are utilized in the management or in the total care of these patients. 

00:21:53.880 --> 00:22:04.210
Childhood cancer is happily not common, but does rank as the second most frequent cause of death in children past the neonatal period. 

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:08.880
With early diagnosis and with skilled and dedicated treatment however,

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:16.850
a favorable outcome may be anticipated in a substantial number of these patients.

